1. What You May Do

You may, at no charge and without asking:

  a. Run the Software, hosted or locally, for any purpose. That includes
     commercial, internal, and educational use, by a person or an organisation,
     with no seat limit.

  b. Keep copies for your own use. Your browser has to download the app to run
     it, and a local clone or a private fork on your own machines or your
     organisation's private infrastructure is fine.

  c. Modify your own copies however you like, and run what you modify under the
     terms above.

  d. Read the source, study it, and learn from it.


2. Your Output Is Yours

The C++ that ImGuiStudio generates, the documents you build, and anything you
export from it belong entirely to you. This license places no condition of any
kind on that output. No attribution, no share-alike, no notice, no royalty. Use
it in closed source, in a product you sell, anywhere.

This section survives termination of the rest of the license, so code you have
already generated stays unencumbered no matter what happens to your right to run
the Software.


3. What You May Not Do

Without prior written permission from the copyright holder, you may not:

  a. Redistribute the Software, in source or compiled form, in whole or in
     substantial part. That covers publishing it, sharing it, bundling it inside
     another product, and uploading it to a package registry or an app store.

  b. Host a public instance of the Software, or offer it to third parties as a
     service, whether or not you charge for it. Running it privately for
     yourself or inside your own organisation is not hosting a public instance.

  c. Sell, rent, lease, sublicense, or otherwise transfer the Software or any
     right in it.

  d. Publish a derivative work of the Software. Private modifications under
     section 1(c) are fine. Handing them to anyone else is not.

  e. Remove, alter, or obscure this license, the copyright notice, or the
     Third-Party Notices file from any copy you hold.

Nothing here restricts rights you have under applicable law that cannot be
waived by contract, such as a statutory right to make a backup or to
decompile for interoperability.


